    "chunk_size_bytes": 1876,
    "chunk_text": "Prices realized by Ceylon Tea at Osaka charity auction enter \u2018Guinness Book of Records\u2019\n\nThe \u2018Asia Siyaka Tea Update\u2019 said that Ceylon Tea at the recent charity auction in Osaka, Japan, garnered resounding successes. The end result was prices entering the \u2018Guinness Book of Records\u2019 for tea. All \u2018marks\u2019 were for Ceylon Tea from plantations in High, Mid Grown and Low Grown elevations. Besides, demand did not falter.\n\nThe Sri Lanka Tea Board and the Colombo Tea Traders\u2019 Association (CTTA) organized the charity auction for Ceylon Tea to instill continued and lively interest in the sector among Regional Plantation Companies and to also win recognition for the smallholder sector, which produces some 75 percent of tea.\n\nThe highest prices were realized by Ruhuna Tea, CTC TEA, Innovative Tea and Green Teas. \u2018These prices would be categorized as unique and perhaps remain so for quite some time. Tea could outshine other industries which currently hold sway as being outstanding forex earners, tea industry sources said.\n\nIn passing it must be mentioned that tea and other plantation crops, for example rubber and its subsidiary products, are recognized as high income earners, continue to earn sizeable forex and by virtue of that fact cannot be seen as of secondary importance to economic wellbeing.\n\nForbes and Walker commenting on the auctions this week said offerings were the lowest on offer since sale no 16 of 22nd and 24th 2024. Ex-estate offerings were maintained at around 0.6M/kgs. Low grown leafy teas met with mixed demand.\n\nLow grown\u2019s totaled approximately 2.1 M/kgs. All categories met with fair demand the report said.\n\nReverting to the Asia Siyaka report, the company said Assam would host India\u2019s first decaffeinated Black Tea plant. The state is poised to house this plant, marking a new chapter in the country\u2019s tea industry."
