    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "Share market dips slightly due to profit-takings but records over Rs 7 billion turnover\n\nThe CSE recorded a 1800 points gain during the last 14 working days but yesterday the market witnessed a drop due to slight profit- taking, market analysts said.\n\nAmid those developments both indices moved downwards. The All Share Price Index went down by 65.24 points, while the S and P SL20 declined by 26.90 points.\n\nTurnover stood at Rs 7.32 billion with 14 crossings. The top seven crossings were; Cargills 881,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 661 million; its shares traded at Rs 750, CT Holdings 728,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 474 million; its shares traded at Rs 651, Commercial Bank two million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 389 million; its shares sold at Rs 196.50, Colombo Dockyards 1.6 million shares crossed for Rs 219 million; its shares traded at Rs 136, Chevron Lubricants one million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 180 ; its shares sold at Rs 180, DFCC 400,000 shares crossed for Rs 65.2 million; its shares traded at Rs 163 and JKH three million shares crossed for Rs 60.2 million\u2019; its shares sold at Rs 21.40.\n\nIn the retail market top seven companies that mainly contributed to the turnover were; Commercial Bank Rs 293 million (1.5 million shares traded), LCB Finance Rs 267 million (32 million shares traded), JKH Rs 267 million (12.4 million shares traded), HNB (Non-Voting) Rs 216 million (700,000 shares traded), Colombo Dockyards Rs 172 million (1.3 million shares traded) Sunshine Holdings Rs 153 million (4.7 million shares traded) and Pan Asia Power Rs 127 million (3.9 million shares traded). During the day 189 million shares volumes changed hands in 38497 transactions.\n\nIt is said that the banking and financial sector performed well at the floor, especially Commercial Bank, while the manufacturing sector, especially Chevron and JKH, was also significantly active in the market."
