    "md5": "b9b6a5025c44abbd631de68452b4fc5b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1705,
    "chunk_text": "Furthermore, unpredictable taxes weaken the trust in government incentives. Even when special tax holidays or investment allowances are announced, investors are skeptical about whether they will last for the duration of their projects. Without consistency, incentives become meaningless, and confidence falters.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s labor laws, though initially meant to protect workers\u2019 rights, have become one of the most rigid and outdated regulatory systems in the region. While social protection is important, excessive regulation makes it very hard for businesses \u2014 both local and foreign \u2014 to operate flexibly and stay competitive. For investors, this rigidity leads to higher costs, lower efficiency, and limited ability to respond quickly to market changes.\n\nForeign investors consistently cite Sri Lanka\u2019s inflexible hiring and firing laws, complex dispute resolution procedures, and the high cost of compliance as major deterrents. The Termination of Employment of Workmen Act (TEWA), for instance, requires prior government approval for dismissing even a single employee, regardless of business necessity. Such provisions discourage new investment and expansion because firms fear they will be unable to adjust their workforce during downturns or technological restructuring.\n\nIn contrast, countries like Vietnam and Indonesia have modernized their labor frameworks to balance protection and productivity\u2014introducing flexible contracts, transparent severance systems, and social safety nets that promote fairness without hindering businesses. Sri Lanka\u2019s failure to adapt in this way has put it at a disadvantage in global manufacturing and services, where agility and speed are crucial."
  },
